{"name":"Flood Tide","hex":"#6c98a2","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"flood tide","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The period between low tide and the next high tide of the sea as the water flows toward the shore.","The highest point of something; a climax."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flood_tide"}],"entries":[{"title":"Flood Tide (1934 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Flood Tide is a 1934 British drama film directed by John Baxter and starring George Carney, Janice Adair and Minnie Rayner. It was written by Anson Dyer and Harry Fowler Mear."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c7/Flood_Tide_film_Opening_titles_%281934%29.jpg/330px-Flood_Tide_film_Opening_titles_%281934%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flood_Tide_(1934_film)"},{"title":"Tide","blocks":[{"text":"Tides are the periodic rise and fall of sea level resulting from the differential gravitational forces exerted primarily by the Moon and the Sun, combined with inertial effects associated with the Earth–Moon system’s orbital motion and the Earth's rotation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Tide_overview.svg/330px-Tide_overview.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tide"},{"title":"Flood","blocks":[{"text":"A flood is an overflow of water that submerges land that is usually dry. In the sense of \"flowing water\", the word may also be applied to the inflow of the tide. Floods are of significant concern in agriculture, civil engineering and public health. Human changes to the environment often increase the intensity and frequency of flooding. Examples for human changes are land use changes such as deforestation and removal of wetlands, changes in waterway course or flood controls such as with levees. Global environmental issues also influence causes of floods, namely climate change which causes an intensification of the water cycle and sea level rise. For example, climate change makes extreme weather events more frequent and stronger. This leads to more intense floods and increased flood risk."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Urban_flood_cropped.jpg/330px-Urban_flood_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flood"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c7/Flood_Tide_film_Opening_titles_%281934%29.jpg/330px-Flood_Tide_film_Opening_titles_%281934%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c7/Flood_Tide_film_Opening_titles_%281934%29.jpg/330px-Flood_Tide_film_Opening_titles_%281934%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlood_Tide_film_Opening_titles_(1934).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/High_tide_sun_moon_same_side_beginning.png/120px-High_tide_sun_moon_same_side_beginning.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/High_tide_sun_moon_same_side_beginning.png/250px-High_tide_sun_moon_same_side_beginning.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/High_tide_sun_moon_same_side_beginning.png/120px-High_tide_sun_moon_same_side_beginning.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHigh_tide_sun_moon_same_side_beginning.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Low_tide_sun_moon_90_degrees.png/120px-Low_tide_sun_moon_90_degrees.png?utm_source=en.wikip